WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.847 --> 00:00:05.421
A drawing shows a part in views: straight from the front, the top and the side,

2
00:00:05.572 --> 00:00:06.675
without perspective.

3
00:00:07.379 --> 00:00:08.737
Here, a bent bracket.

4
00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:11.996
Europe uses first-angle projection:

5
00:00:12.112 --> 00:00:14.991
what you see from the top goes below the front view,

6
00:00:15.119 --> 00:00:17.499
what you see from the left goes on the right.

7
00:00:18.528 --> 00:00:23.009
The United States uses third-angle projection: the top view sits above,

8
00:00:23.207 --> 00:00:25.703
and on the right goes what you see from the right.

9
00:00:26.727 --> 00:00:30.187
A small cone symbol in the title block tells you which.

10
00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:33.899
Mix them up and you make the part as a mirror image.

11
00:00:35.245 --> 00:00:40.423
A section view shows the inside: you cut the part in your mind along the line A-A.

12
00:00:41.157 --> 00:00:45.348
Hatching, thin diagonal lines, marks the material that was cut.

13
00:00:46.381 --> 00:00:50.061
The 3D picture in the corner is for orientation only:

14
00:00:50.247 --> 00:00:52.186
you never take dimensions from it.

15
00:00:53.212 --> 00:00:58.540
The scale says how big it is drawn: one to one is true size, one to two half size,

16
00:00:58.738 --> 00:00:59.725
two to one double.

17
00:01:00.739 --> 00:01:03.200
Never measure off a print: paper stretches.

18
00:01:03.938 --> 00:01:09.476
At a scale of one to five, 40 millimetres on paper is 200 in real life,

19
00:01:09.603 --> 00:01:12.413
but just read the dimension written beside it.

20
00:01:13.747 --> 00:01:18.078
You start at the title block, the box at the bottom right: part number,

21
00:01:18.275 --> 00:01:22.258
name, material, scale, projection symbol, who drew and approved it,

22
00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:23.756
and the revision letter.

23
00:01:24.779 --> 00:01:31.408
This is a bracket in S235, a common structural steel, at one to one, revision C.

24
00:01:32.388 --> 00:01:34.350
The general tolerance is here too:

25
00:01:34.512 --> 00:01:37.670
it covers every dimension without its own tolerance.

26
00:01:38.381 --> 00:01:41.598
Here ISO 2768, class m.

27
00:01:42.609 --> 00:01:46.533
A dimension of 50 millimetres, like the depth of this bracket,

28
00:01:46.696 --> 00:01:49.993
may then be 49.7 to 50.3.

29
00:01:50.672 --> 00:01:54.619
If it must be tighter, the dimension gets a tolerance of its own.

30
00:01:55.944 --> 00:01:59.067
Every change gets a new revision, with a new letter.

31
00:01:59.779 --> 00:02:02.519
The revision table says what changed and when.

32
00:02:03.537 --> 00:02:07.786
Revision B made the hole 9 millimetres instead of 8.5.

33
00:02:08.501 --> 00:02:14.759
Revision C, in January 2025, made the bend radius 3 instead of 2.

34
00:02:15.747 --> 00:02:18.139
But at the press brake the setter worked from

35
00:02:18.185 --> 00:02:20.972
a printed revision B in a plastic sleeve:

36
00:02:21.123 --> 00:02:23.607
400 brackets with the old radius,

37
00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:25.302
rejected at the customer.

38
00:02:26.327 --> 00:02:29.775
The fix is not being more careful, but a screen at the machine,

39
00:02:29.868 --> 00:02:31.714
straight from the PDM system.

40
00:02:32.485 --> 00:02:35.562
Then only the current revision exists on the floor.

41
00:02:36.893 --> 00:02:41.560
A plus-or-minus tolerance controls only size: this 10.2 hole,

42
00:02:41.711 --> 00:02:43.325
zero to plus one tenth.

43
00:02:44.188 --> 00:02:48.078
Where it sits and what form it has, plus or minus does not say.

44
00:02:49.052 --> 00:02:51.467
That is what geometric tolerances are for,

45
00:02:51.629 --> 00:02:55.205
in a feature control frame: a row of boxes with a symbol,

46
00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:57.110
a tolerance and the datums.

47
00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:04.451
Read it from left to right: position, within a round zone 0.3 across, the circled M,

48
00:03:04.613 --> 00:03:06.738
and the datums A, B and C.

49
00:03:07.701 --> 00:03:11.103
Europe and the United States read some drawings differently:

50
00:03:11.312 --> 00:03:14.203
ask first which standard the drawing invokes.

51
00:03:15.527 --> 00:03:19.080
You measure from a datum: the surface the part is located on,

52
00:03:19.242 --> 00:03:21.506
drawn as a small triangle with a letter.

53
00:03:22.224 --> 00:03:23.791
Think of the corner of a room.

54
00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:27.172
Primary is A, here the bottom face.

55
00:03:27.873 --> 00:03:32.203
The plate rests on three points, because three points define a plane.

56
00:03:33.188 --> 00:03:36.636
Secondary is B, the long edge, against two pins.

57
00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:40.788
Tertiary is C, the short edge, against one pin.

58
00:03:41.795 --> 00:03:45.301
The order matters: that is how the part sits in the fixture,

59
00:03:45.533 --> 00:03:47.739
and how the measuring machine aligns it.

60
00:03:48.499 --> 00:03:51.239
Measure from another edge and you get another number.

61
00:03:52.539 --> 00:03:56.823
The plate has four holes of 10.2 millimetres for M10 bolts.

62
00:03:57.537 --> 00:03:58.593
Where must they sit?

63
00:03:59.651 --> 00:04:01.381
A basic dimension says that,

64
00:04:01.543 --> 00:04:07.719
in a box without plus or minus: this hole sits exactly 25 from B and 40 from C.

65
00:04:08.709 --> 00:04:11.855
The tolerance is in the frame: the position tolerance.

66
00:04:12.573 --> 00:04:18.215
The centre of the hole must lie within a round zone 0.3 across, around that spot.

67
00:04:19.273 --> 00:04:19.912
Why round?

68
00:04:20.646 --> 00:04:23.224
A bolt can shift the same amount every way.

69
00:04:23.999 --> 00:04:27.506
A circle of 0.28 around a square of plus or

70
00:04:27.575 --> 00:04:30.803
minus one tenth has 57 percent more area:

71
00:04:30.954 --> 00:04:32.742
more good parts get accepted.

72
00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:36.321
You do not check position with a caliper.

73
00:04:37.082 --> 00:04:41.749
A CMM, a coordinate measuring machine, aligns on A, B and C,

74
00:04:41.854 --> 00:04:44.223
probes the hole and computes the centre.

75
00:04:45.238 --> 00:04:53.075
The centre is 0.12 off in X and 0.09 in Y: 0.15 millimetres together.

76
00:04:53.795 --> 00:04:57.731
As a diameter that is 0.30, right on the limit.

77
00:04:58.739 --> 00:05:00.898
But the circled M means MMC,

78
00:05:01.049 --> 00:05:04.822
maximum material condition: the size with the most material,

79
00:05:04.973 --> 00:05:06.529
for a hole the smallest hole.

80
00:05:07.505 --> 00:05:13.530
This hole measures 10.25: 0.05 bigger, and it gets that as a bonus.

81
00:05:14.228 --> 00:05:18.199
The zone grows to 0.35, and the part is good.
